        "id": "jBA0U1D82FfN"
      },
      "source": [
        "**Question**. Does processing time decreases if you add the option `num_workers=2` when defining the dataloader? Compute training time with and without that option and show the results for comparison.\n",
        "\n",
        "\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "code",
      "execution_count": 40,
      "metadata": {},
      "outputs": [
        {
          "name": "stdout",
          "output_type": "stream",
          "text": [
            "Training time comparison\n",
            "num_workers=0: 25.13 seconds\n",
            "num_workers=2: failed (DataLoader worker (pid(s) 26384, 11440) exited unexpectedly)\n",
            "num_workers=2 could not be timed in this environment, so num_workers=0 is the safer setting here.\n"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "source": [
        "# Compare training time with different DataLoader worker settings\n",
        "def train_for_timing(num_workers, epochs=3):\n",
        "    timing_loader = DataLoader(\n",
        "        train_dataset,\n",
        "        batch_size=batch_size,\n",
        "        shuffle=True,\n",
        "        num_workers=num_workers\n",
        "    )\n",
        "\n",
        "    timing_model = SimpleNN(input_size, hidden_size, num_classes)\n",
        "    timing_criterion = nn.CrossEntropyLoss()\n",
        "    timing_optimizer = optim.Adam(timing_model.parameters(), lr=0.001)\n",
        "\n",
        "    start_time = time.perf_counter()\n",
        "    timing_model.train()\n",
        "    for epoch in range(epochs):\n",
        "        for images, labels in timing_loader:\n",
        "            outputs = timing_model(images)\n",
        "            loss = timing_criterion(outputs, labels)\n",
        "            timing_optimizer.zero_grad()\n",
        "            loss.backward()\n",
        "            timing_optimizer.step()\n",
        "    return time.perf_counter() - start_time\n",
        "\n",
        "worker_options = [0, 2]\n",
        "timing_results = []\n",
        "\n",
        "for workers in worker_options:\n",
        "    try:\n",
        "        elapsed = train_for_timing(num_workers=workers, epochs=num_epochs)\n",
        "        timing_results.append({\n",
        "            \"num_workers\": workers,\n",
        "            \"training_time_seconds\": elapsed,\n",
        "            \"error\": None\n",
        "        })\n",
        "    except RuntimeError as error:\n",
        "        timing_results.append({\n",
        "            \"num_workers\": workers,\n",
        "            \"training_time_seconds\": None,\n",
        "            \"error\": str(error).split(\"\\n\")[0]\n",
        "        })\n",
        "\n",
        "print(\"Training time comparison\")\n",
        "for result in timing_results:\n",
        "    if result[\"error\"] is None:\n",
        "        print(f\"num_workers={result['num_workers']}: {result['training_time_seconds']:.2f} seconds\")\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        print(f\"num_workers={result['num_workers']}: failed ({result['error']})\")\n",
        "\n",
        "time_0 = timing_results[0][\"training_time_seconds\"]\n",
        "time_2 = timing_results[1][\"training_time_seconds\"]\n",
        "if time_0 is not None and time_2 is not None:\n",
        "    if time_2 < time_0:\n",
        "        print(f\"num_workers=2 was faster by {time_0 - time_2:.2f} seconds.\")\n",
        "    else:\n",
        "        print(f\"num_workers=2 was not faster. It was slower by {time_2 - time_0:.2f} seconds.\")\n",
        "else:\n",
        "    print(\"num_workers=2 could not be timed in this environment, so num_workers=0 is the safer setting here.\")\n"
      ]
    },
    {
      "cell_type": "markdown",
      "metadata": {},
      "source": [
        "**Answer.**\n",
        "- I compared the training time with different dataloader settings. I tested `num_workers=0` and `num_workers=2`.\n"
        "- In my Colab run, `num_workers=0` completed in 28.58 seconds, while `num_workers=2` completed in 30.78 seconds. So `num_workers=2` was not faster. It was actually slower by about 2.19 seconds.\n"
        "- This means that, in this environment, adding extra dataloader workers did not decrease the processing time. For this small 8 by 8 MNIST example, the overhead of creating and managing extra worker processes was probably larger than the benefit of parallel data loading.\n"
        "- So, based on this experiment, `num_workers=0` is the better setting for this notebook in Colab.\n"
      ]
    },
    {
